  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:Wj_CPdb6ddk",
    "purpose": "Explains the history of American hunting jackets and walks through the construction details of several vintage examples, ending with an answer to why old hunting jacket sleeves look so short.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Vintage hunting jackets are so cleverly built that people assume every detail is deliberate. Their popularity grew out of 'frontier anxiety' and the rise of the American city. The short sleeves are most likely shrinkage from heavy washing rather than design.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Common theories for short hunting-jacket sleeves (shooting, shorter people, gloves) are dismissed. Old catalogs suggest sleeves weren't short originally, and heavy washing likely shrank them.",
      "American hunting jackets grew from an open, everyone-hunts culture and favour roomy cuts. English jackets like the Norfolk reflect restrictive, wealthy hunting culture and suit-like tailoring.",
      "An L.L.Bean sportsman jacket with a canvas game bag but no rubber is argued to be 1940s (WWII rubber rationing) rather than the commonly assumed 1930s.",
      "A pivot sleeve, rubberized game pouches, high hand pockets for carrying a broken shotgun, and shell pockets are typical features shown.",
      "Frontier anxiety after the frontier closed, plus Teddy Roosevelt's 'strenuous life', drove the boom in hunting clothing from brands like Duxbak and L.L.Bean.",
      "Duxbak was a premium brand. Its construction details include a one-piece folded shoulder, full-length facing, a two-layer upper sleeve, bound seams and a 'ducktail' to reduce bulk.",
      "Duxbak declined with overseas production and nylon. Ralph Lauren and later Buck Mason revived the look as nostalgia."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The short-sleeve mystery",
        "summary": "Opens with the question of why hunting jacket sleeves are always short, lists online theories, and sets up a look at what makes these jackets special.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Why hunting jackets got popular; the Pontiac jacket",
        "summary": "Previews the idea that hunting jackets exploded in the early 1900s out of male anxiety. Shows a 1950s Pontiac jacket as a cheap archetype, with its action back, simple sleeves and a clever shared pocket flap.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "English hunting culture and the Norfolk jacket",
        "summary": "Contrasts restrictive, wealthy English hunting with American hunting, and explains the Norfolk jacket as suit-like utilitarian tailoring.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "L.L.Bean sportsman jacket",
        "summary": "Examines an L.L.Bean sportsman jacket, its game bag and missing rubber, argues for a 1940s date due to rubber rationing, and demonstrates its pivot sleeve.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Hunting smocks and American identity",
        "summary": "Covers indigenous influence on hunting gear and the linen hunting smock in the Revolutionary War. Explains how the hunter became core to American identity until the frontier closed.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsor: Cometeer",
        "summary": "Sponsor read for Cometeer frozen coffee pucks.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 15
      },
      {
        "title": "Frontier anxiety",
        "summary": "Explains how the closing of the frontier in the census created 'frontier anxiety' and set off the outdoors craze.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "1930s Red Head hunting jacket",
        "summary": "Shows a rubber-coated Red Head jacket with lined hand pockets, a modified pivot sleeve and a rubberized game pouch. Explains why hand pockets sit high.",
        "first_segment": 18,
        "last_segment": 19
      },
      {
        "title": "Teddy Roosevelt and the strenuous life",
        "summary": "Covers Roosevelt's influence, the strenuous life, railroad vacations to places like Maine, and the market that opened for outdoor clothing.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 22
      },
      {
        "title": "Duxbak history and the Norfolk paradox",
        "summary": "Introduces Duxbak (founded 1904) as a premium hunting brand that also sold Norfolk jackets. Argues the era really marks the rise of the American city.",
        "first_segment": 22,
        "last_segment": 25
      },
      {
        "title": "Duxbak jacket construction",
        "summary": "Details a Duxbak jacket: coated canvas, a folded one-piece shoulder, half-moon detail, full facing, two-layer upper sleeve and catalog features like the pack back. Compares seam choices with Filson's Mackinaw and shows bulk-reducing seam work.",
        "first_segment": 26,
        "last_segment": 31
      },
      {
        "title": "Answer: why sleeves are short",
        "summary": "Concludes from old catalogs that sleeves weren't originally short and were likely shrunk by heavy washing.",
        "first_segment": 31,
        "last_segment": 32
      },
      {
        "title": "Duxbak's decline and the revival",
        "summary": "Explains Duxbak's decline due to overseas production and nylon. Notes Ralph Lauren and Buck Mason reviving the style as a cycle of nostalgia.",
        "first_segment": 32,
        "last_segment": 34
      }
    ],
